dn't work so great it's not great  because my family was not at peace  at the age of nine defranco met a local  musician  who introduced her to the buffalo folk  scene at 15 she moved out of the house  and by the early 1990s wound up in new  york city where she became an indie star  our apartment was the fifth floor there  was i think  eight of us that live there do you ever  just want to give up  sixteen years ago she made the move to  new orleans finding motherhood  and a second marriage you've had  difficulties in your marriage  and you've been open about them marriage  is hard  can be and uh it was really difficult  uh for my fella for a lot of years when  the kids were little  and he was holding down the fort all by  himself  and you know you you are struggling  in these different spheres and you grow  apart  and resentment build but this  album this new album of mine is sort of  like  the breakup album without the breakup  we made it through  [Music]  defranco has produced and distributed  every single one of her albums  independently on righteous babe records  has there ever been a moment when you've  regretted not doing a major label  studio album i don't  think so in working with a big time  producer  well it's hard to know about the road  not taken right  you know actually  i changed my answer i've regretted it a  lot  and thought along the way that is a more  true answer  i look back at some of the things that  i've recorded  and i think dang those are some good  songs and i didn't do them justice and  if only i had brought in the team of  professionals  you know i was just a kid with a  stubborn stubborn kid who was just like  i'm not going to wait around for the  team of professionals i'm going to do it  it's not perfect but hey but sometimes i  do look back at my records and i think  sorry songs  [Music]  defranco's brutal honesty is something  her dedicated fans have seen for more  than three decades  and it sounds like there is much more to  come  [Music]  i hope that i have helped more than i've  harmed  it always felt ironic that i was  described again and again and again for  decades as an  angry woman you know because it's an  easy label  yeah but i feel like i've always come  from that place of revolutionary love i  love  my country and that's why i fight for it  in my way you know it's all out of  out of love

